CommitDate: Thu Oct 15 11:19:23 2026 +0800

    [Bugfix] Synchronize NPU worker device (#17666)
    
    ### What this PR does / why we need it?
    
    Ports the Ascend backend requirement from vLLM
    [#52914](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/52914): a resolved
    pause
    promises an idle device, and backends outside `torch.accelerator` must
    provide
    their own wait.
    
    Ascend provided none. `pause_generation()` could return while the last
    dummy
    batch a DP rank launched was still executing, and `wake_up()` left its
    tail work
    (the level-2 buffer restores and the sleep-mode cleanup) unwaited -- so
    a caller
    starting a weight sync right after wake-up raced it.
    
    This adds `NPUWorker.synchronize_device()`, which blocks on
    `torch.npu.synchronize()`, and ends `wake_up()` with it. The method
    stays on the
    worker rather than relying on inheritance because `wake_up` calls it
    unconditionally, while vLLM releases predating #52914 define
    no `WorkerBase.synchronize_device`.
    
    Verified against the vLLM tree (`vllm/v1/worker/worker_base.py`): the
    method is
    **absent in v0.28.0** and **present from v0.29.0 onward**. v0.28.0 is in
    the
    support matrix, so on that line the override is the only barrier that
    exists --
    there is nothing to inherit, and `wake_up()` would otherwise raise
    `AttributeError`.
    
    `sleep()` gets the same treatment, bracketing the memory transition with
    a
    barrier on each side: one before the baseline `mem_get_info()` snapshot
    that the
    freed-bytes assertion depends on, and one after the optional RL extra
    cleanup --
    whose `destroy_hccl()`/aclgraph work is the last thing that can touch
    memory
    before the allocator unmaps it. Without them, `sleep()` racing an
    in-flight
    dummy batch fails inside `CaMemAllocator.sleep()`'s `empty_cache()`
    (`AclrtSynchronizeDeviceWithTimeout`, `507011`/`507015`) -- the same
    failure the
    `releases/v0.30.0` fix in #18338 addresses.
    
    ### Does this PR introduce _any_ user-facing change?
    
    No. Control-plane only; no effect on model output.
    
    ### How was this patch tested?
    
    Unit tests (`tests/ut/worker/test_worker_v1.py`): every `wake_up` test
    now patches
    the barrier and asserts it is reached, `synchronize_device()` delegates
    to
    `torch.npu.synchronize()`, and the method is asserted to live on
    `NPUWorker`
    itself.
    
    Unit tests (`tests/ut/worker/test_kv_cache_allocation_scope.py`) and
    `test_sleep_uses_rl_extra_cleanup`: the sleep tests now patch the
    barrier and
    assert both sleep barriers fire, covering the cleanup-enabled and
    cleanup-disabled paths. They also stop executing a real
    `torch.npu.synchronize()`
    on NPU hosts, which previously made these specs behave differently on
    hardware
    versus CPU runners.
    
    NPU system tests:
    
    -
    `tests/e2e/pull_request/one_card/rlhf/state_transitions/test_npu_device_synchronize.py`
    -- on real hardware, `synchronize_device()` and `wake_up()` must leave
    the NPU
      stream idle.
    -
    `tests/e2e/pull_request/two_card/rlhf/state_transitions/test_pause_device_idle.py`
    -- the Ascend port of upstream's
    `test_dp_pause_completion_implies_device_idle`.
    In DPLB the pause path itself launches the dummy batch whose completion
    the pause
    promise covers, so the test inflates the dummy batches the pause
    consensus
    manufactures and requires an idle device the moment `pause_generation()`
    returns.
    It skips (rather than passing vacuously) on vLLM without the #52914
    barrier.
    -
    `tests/e2e/pull_request/one_card/rlhf/state_transitions/npu_stream_busy.py`
      -- the device-busy helper both files share.
    
    `torch.cuda._sleep(200_000_000)`, which the upstream test uses to keep a
    CUDA
    stream busy, cannot run on NPU: `torch.npu` exposes no `_sleep`, and
    `torch.cuda._sleep` raises `AttributeError: module 'torch._C' has no
    attribute
    '_cuda_sleep'`. `enqueue_npu_busy_work()` queues real matmuls without
    waiting
    instead, and `torch.npu.current_stream().query()` reports the same "is
    the stream
    quiet" fact as `torch.cuda.current_stream().query()`.
    
    Re-verified on Ascend 910B3 (2 chips) against the vLLM main lane
    (`0.30.0`, the pinned `.github/vllm-main-verified.commit`), i.e. after
    the sleep
    barriers were added -- 84 passed:
    
    | suite | result |
    |---|---|
    | `tests/ut/worker/test_worker_v1.py` +
    `tests/ut/worker/test_kv_cache_allocation_scope.py` | 81 passed |
    |
    `tests/e2e/pull_request/one_card/rlhf/state_transitions/test_npu_device_synchronize.py`
    | 2 passed |
    |
    `tests/e2e/pull_request/two_card/rlhf/state_transitions/test_pause_device_idle.py`
    | 1 passed (DP=2 engine, not skipped) |
    
    The DP pause test uses `Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct`, a small text-only
    checkpoint
    the a3 runners already pre-download. CI runs with
    `VLLM_USE_MODELSCOPE=1` and
    `HF_HUB_OFFLINE=1`, so an unlisted checkpoint cannot be fetched on the
    runners.
    
    An earlier A/B check of the pause barrier itself, run on the vLLM 0.29.0
    line
    before the sleep barriers were added, kept a DP=2 engine busy for ~200
    ms and then
    called `pause_generation()`:
    
    | | fixed | barrier neutralised |
    |---|---|---|
    | `pause_generation()` wall time | 0.229 s | 0.036 s |
    | device idle when it returned | True | **False** |
    
    ### Upstream context
    
    Ports the Ascend backend requirement from vLLM
    [#52914](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/52914).
    
    - vLLM main:
    https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/commit/8cc9aa5ad350f6f80cf3bebb298d410803ff75a9
    
    Signed-off-by: SeaAsters <keine214@qq.com>
